      body: `## The collapse of the Reich

From 1943 the war turned decisively against Germany. Allied bombing destroyed German cities: the firestorm in **Hamburg** in July 1943 killed some 35,000–40,000 people in a few days, and the raids on **Dresden** in February 1945 some 25,000. In all, about 350,000–400,000 German civilians died in the air war. On **June 6, 1944**, Allied forces landed in **Normandy**; Paris was liberated on August 25. In the East, the Red Army advanced across Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, and Hungary.

## July 20, 1944

Within Germany, a small conservative and military resistance had plotted against Hitler since 1938. On **July 20, 1944**, Colonel **Claus Schenk Graf von Stauffenberg**, a wounded veteran and devout Catholic aristocrat, placed a bomb in a briefcase under the conference table at Hitler's headquarters in East Prussia. It exploded, but a heavy table support shielded Hitler, who suffered only minor injuries. The coup in Berlin collapsed within hours. Stauffenberg was shot that night; some 7,000 people were arrested, and about 200 were executed, many after show trials before Roland Freisler's People's Court. The conspirators' motives were mixed—some had served the regime loyally and had been complicit in its crimes, some had acted from Christian conscience, and many wished to save Germany from total defeat—but their action became a foundation of post-war German democratic memory. Earlier, in February 1943, the students Hans and Sophie Scholl of the **White Rose** group in Munich had been executed for distributing leaflets denouncing the regime's crimes.

## Warsaw, 1944

On **August 1, 1944**, as the Red Army approached Warsaw, the Polish Home Army rose against the Germans, hoping to liberate the capital before the Soviets arrived and to assert Polish independence. The Red Army halted on the eastern bank of the Vistula, and Stalin refused for weeks to allow Allied aircraft to use Soviet airfields to supply the insurgents. After sixty-three days of fighting, the Home Army surrendered on October 2. Some 150,000 to 200,000 Polish civilians were killed, many in massacres such as those in the Wola district, where tens of thousands were shot in the first days. Hitler then ordered the city razed; by January 1945, when the Soviets entered, most of Warsaw had been systematically destroyed.

## The Red Army in Germany

As the Red Army entered German territory in East Prussia in the autumn and winter of 1944–1945, it brought with it the memory of everything the Germans had done in the Soviet Union, encouraged by propaganda that called for vengeance. Soviet soldiers committed mass violence against German civilians, above all mass rape: historians estimate that hundreds of thousands, perhaps as many as two million, German women were raped in 1945, in East Prussia, Silesia, and Berlin. Millions of Germans fled west in the bitter winter of 1945, many on foot across the frozen lagoons of the Baltic coast; the sinking of the refugee ship *Wilhelm Gustloff* by a Soviet submarine on January 30, 1945, killed some 9,000 people, the greatest loss of life in a single sinking in maritime history.

At **Yalta** in February 1945, Roosevelt, Churchill, and Stalin agreed on the occupation of Germany in zones, on the shifting of Poland's borders westward, and on "free elections" in liberated Europe—a promise Stalin would not keep. For the peoples of eastern Europe, the end of the war brought one occupation in place of another.

## The end

As the Allies closed in, the regime turned its violence inward and onto its prisoners. Hundreds of thousands of concentration-camp inmates were driven westward on **death marches** in the winter of 1944–1945; perhaps a third of the some 700,000 prisoners still in the camps in January 1945 died before liberation. Soviet troops entered **Auschwitz** on **January 27, 1945**, finding some 7,000 surviving prisoners. American and British troops liberated Buchenwald, Dachau, and **Bergen-Belsen** in April, where they found tens of thousands of starving prisoners and thousands of unburied dead. The BBC correspondent Richard Dimbleby's report from Belsen shocked the British public.

In Berlin, as Soviet forces fought their way into the city, Hitler committed suicide in his bunker on **April 30, 1945**. Goebbels and his wife killed their six children and themselves the next day. Germany surrendered unconditionally on May 7–8, 1945. In Europe, the war had killed some 40 million people.

## The reckoning at Nuremberg

The victors resolved to try Germany's leaders. The **International Military Tribunal** at Nuremberg, established by the United States, the Soviet Union, Britain, and France, tried twenty-two leading figures from November 1945 to October 1946 on four counts: conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes, and **crimes against humanity**—a new category in international law. Twelve defendants, including Göring, Ribbentrop, Frank, and Keitel, were sentenced to death; Göring killed himself before his execution. Three were acquitted, among them Papen. The trials established a documentary record of the regime's crimes and the principle that individuals, including heads of state, are responsible for crimes under international law, and that obedience to orders is no defense. Subsequent trials dealt with doctors, judges, industrialists, generals, and Einsatzgruppen commanders.

The legal scholar **Raphael Lemkin**, a Polish Jewish refugee who had lost most of his family in the Holocaust, coined the word **genocide** in 1944 to describe "the destruction of a nation or of an ethnic group." His campaign led to the United Nations **Genocide Convention** of December 9, 1948.

Nuremberg had its limits. One of the judging powers, the Soviet Union, had itself committed crimes in the same years, and its prosecutors tried to charge the Germans with the Katyn massacre—a charge quietly dropped. Most perpetrators were never tried; many returned to careers in West and East Germany. The first major German-led trials of Auschwitz personnel, in Frankfurt from 1963 to 1965, began a new phase of reckoning.

## Expulsions and a new order

The end of the war brought new displacements. Between 1944 and 1950, some **12 million Germans** fled or were expelled from Poland, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, and other eastern territories; hundreds of thousands died. Poland's borders were moved west, and Poles were moved from the territories annexed by the Soviet Union into former German lands. Millions of displaced persons—forced laborers, prisoners, survivors—sought to go home or to find new homes; many Jewish survivors, finding their communities destroyed and facing new violence such as the pogrom in Kielce, Poland, in July 1946, left for Palestine and the United States.

The defeat of Nazi Germany did not end totalitarian rule in Europe. Across eastern and central Europe, the Red Army's presence allowed communist parties to take power between 1945 and 1948, establishing regimes modeled on the Soviet Union. For the peoples between Germany and Russia, 1945 was a liberation from Nazism, but not, in most cases, a liberation into freedom.`,
